-- Q4010: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A gift from a grandparent to an adult child of the grandparent","A direct gift to a grandchild whose parent is still living","A transfer between spouses regardless of the amount","A charitable contribution to a tax-exempt organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 2612(a), the GSTT applies to transfers to skip persons — individuals two or more generations below the transferor. A grandchild whose parent (the transferor''s child) is still alive is a skip person under Sec. 2613(a), making the direct gift subject to GSTT.
Wrong (A): A gift to an adult child is a transfer to a non-skip person one generation below, so no GSTT applies.
Wrong (C): Spouses are of the same generation, so transfers between them are not generation-skipping.
Wrong (D): Charitable transfers are not subject to GSTT because charities are not skip persons.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4010;

-- Q4013: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000, the fair market value at the date of the gift","$250,000, the donor''s adjusted basis in the property","$225,000, the average of fair market value and basis","$0, because the property was received as a gift"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 1015(a) and Treas. Reg. Sec. 1.1015-1(a)(2), when gifted property has an FMV less than the donor''s adjusted basis, the donee uses dual basis rules. For determining a loss, the donee''s basis is the FMV at the date of the gift ($200,000). For gain purposes, the donor''s basis ($250,000) applies.
Wrong (B): The donor''s adjusted basis is used only for determining gain, not loss, under the dual basis rules.
Wrong (C): The Code does not average FMV and basis; the rules use one or the other depending on whether a gain or loss results.
Wrong (D): A zero basis does not apply to gifted property; donees receive a carryover or FMV basis under Sec. 1015.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4013;

-- Q4017: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$0 because the annuity returns all contributed value to the grantor","Only the annual gift exclusion amount allocated per remainder beneficiary","The full $3,000,000 contributed to the trust at its inception","The excess appreciation above the 5% Section 7520 assumed return rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In a zeroed-out GRAT, the annuity returns the contributed value plus the Section 7520 assumed return to the grantor. Any appreciation exceeding the 7520 rate passes to remainder beneficiaries transfer-tax-free. With 12% actual growth versus 5% assumed, the excess 7% annual appreciation passes without gift or estate tax.
Wrong (A): A zeroed-out GRAT returns the original value, but excess appreciation above the 7520 rate still passes to beneficiaries.
Wrong (B): The annual gift exclusion is irrelevant to GRATs; the taxable gift is the remainder interest value at inception, which is near zero.
Wrong (C): The full $3,000,000 is returned to the grantor through annuity payments, not transferred to beneficiaries.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4017;

-- Q4019: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An immediate deduction for the full $80,000 with no capital gains tax owed","A deduction for the present value of the remainder interest with deferred gain recognition","No charitable deduction until the trust terminates and the charity receives the assets","A capital loss deduction for the difference between the stock''s basis and FMV"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 664, a CRT is tax-exempt under Sec. 664(c), allowing it to sell appreciated property without triggering immediate capital gains tax to the donor. The donor receives a current deduction under Sec. 170(a) equal to the present value of the charitable remainder interest. Gain is recognized to the beneficiary as distributions are made under the four-tier system of Sec. 664(b).
Wrong (A): The deduction is limited to the present value of the remainder interest, not the full FMV, and gain is eventually recognized through distributions.
Wrong (C): The donor receives a current income tax deduction at the time of contribution, not upon trust termination.
Wrong (D): There is no capital loss — the stock is appreciated (FMV exceeds basis).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4019;

-- Q4021: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The present value of the remainder interest must equal at least 10% of the initial trust assets","At least 10% of the annual trust income must be distributed to charity each year","The trust must distribute a minimum of 10% of its total assets each year","The charity must receive at least 10% of the trust corpus upon termination of the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 664(d), the present value of the charitable remainder interest must be at least 10% of the net fair market value of property placed in the trust at contribution. This is calculated using IRS actuarial tables and the Section 7520 rate. If the test is not met, the trust does not qualify as a CRT.
Wrong (B): The 10% test applies to the present value of the remainder, not to annual income distributions to charity.
Wrong (C): CRTs distribute a fixed annuity or unitrust percentage to the income beneficiary, not 10% of assets annually.
Wrong (D): The test measures the present value of the remainder at inception, not the actual amount received at termination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4021;

-- Q4027: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The grandchild is reclassified as a non-skip person when the intervening parent predeceased the transferor","The exception applies only to testamentary transfers at death, not to lifetime gifts","The exception eliminates all GSTT liability on any transfer to any grandchild","The exception is available only when the receiving grandchild is under the age of 21"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 2651(e), if a transferor''s child (the grandchild''s parent) is deceased at the time of transfer, the grandchild moves up one generation for GSTT purposes. This reclassifies the grandchild as a non-skip person, eliminating GSTT on the transfer. The exception applies to both lifetime gifts and transfers at death.
Wrong (B): The predeceased parent exception applies to both inter vivos gifts and testamentary transfers under Sec. 2651(e).
Wrong (C): The exception only applies when the specific parent in the chain has predeceased, not to all transfers to all grandchildren.
Wrong (D): There is no age limitation on the predeceased parent exception; it depends solely on whether the intervening parent is deceased.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4027;

-- Q4031: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Income and corpus must be distributable before age 21, with remaining assets passing to the minor at 21","The trust must distribute all income to the minor beneficiary on an annual basis","The trust must terminate and distribute all assets when the minor reaches age 18","The minor beneficiary must be appointed to serve as the sole trustee of the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 2503(c), a minor''s trust qualifies gifts as present interests if the property and income may be expended for the minor before age 21, unexpended property passes to the minor at 21, and if the minor dies before 21, the property is payable to the minor''s estate. Unlike Crummey trusts, no withdrawal right is needed.
Wrong (B): Section 2503(c) does not require annual income distribution; it requires that income may be expended for the minor, not that it must be distributed.
Wrong (C): The statutory age under Section 2503(c) is 21, not 18.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement that the minor serve as trustee; the trust is managed by an appointed fiduciary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4031;

-- Q4043: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because all traditional IRA contributions are automatically fully deductible","No, because the deduction is phased out for active participants above the AGI threshold","No, the contribution is entirely nondeductible regardless of the AGI level","Yes, because the amount contributed is within the annual contribution limit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 219(g), single taxpayers who are active participants in an employer plan have their IRA deduction phased out. At $95,000 MAGI, the taxpayer exceeds the phase-out ceiling, so the deduction is fully eliminated. The $7,500 can still be made as a nondeductible contribution under Sec. 408(o).
Wrong (A): IRA deductions are not automatic for active participants; income-based phase-outs apply under Sec. 219(g).
Wrong (C): The contribution is nondeductible at this AGI level, but this answer incorrectly states AGI is irrelevant — the phase-out is AGI-dependent.
Wrong (D): Being within the contribution limit does not guarantee deductibility; the deduction depends on AGI and plan participation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4043;

-- Q4046: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entire $15,000 distribution is received completely tax-free and penalty-free","The $10,000 is tax-free and the $5,000 of earnings is taxable without any penalty","The entire $15,000 distribution is fully taxable as ordinary income with a penalty","The $10,000 of contributions is tax-free but the $5,000 of earnings is taxable with penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 408A(d)(4), Roth IRA distributions follow ordering rules: contributions come out first, tax-free and penalty-free. The $10,000 of contributions is recovered without tax. The $5,000 of earnings is not a qualified distribution because the account has not met the five-year holding period under Sec. 408A(d)(2)(B) and the taxpayer is under 59½, so it is taxable and subject to the 10% penalty under Sec. 72(t).
Wrong (A): Only contributions are tax-free; earnings are taxable because the distribution is not qualified.
Wrong (B): The $5,000 of earnings is subject to both income tax and the 10% early withdrawal penalty, not just income tax.
Wrong (C): Contributions are always recoverable tax-free since they were made with after-tax dollars.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4046;

-- Q4050: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A flat annual maximum of $70,000 regardless of compensation history","A flat annual maximum of $175,000 regardless of compensation history","A flat annual maximum of $275,000 regardless of compensation history","The lesser of 100% of highest 3-year average compensation or $285,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 415(b), the maximum annual benefit from a defined benefit plan is the lesser of 100% of the participant''s average compensation for the highest 3 consecutive years or the annual dollar limit ($285,000 for 2026, indexed for inflation). Benefits beginning before age 62 are actuarially reduced.
Wrong (A): $70,000 is far below the statutory limit and does not correspond to any Section 415 threshold.
Wrong (B): $175,000 is not the correct dollar limit; the 2026 indexed amount is $285,000.
Wrong (C): $275,000 is close but not the correct indexed amount; the actual 2026 limit is $285,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4050;

-- Q4060: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$7,500 because the full conversion amount is treated as taxable income","$3,750 because only half the converted amount is subject to income tax","The conversion is not permitted under current IRS rules for this taxpayer","$0 because the nondeductible contribution already has an after-tax basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): This is the backdoor Roth IRA strategy. The $7,500 contribution was nondeductible (after-tax), so the account has a basis of $7,500 and no earnings. The conversion amount equals the basis, resulting in $0 taxable income. The pro-rata rule under Section 408(d)(2) would apply if the taxpayer had other pre-tax IRA balances.
Wrong (A): The full $7,500 is not taxable because it was a nondeductible contribution with an after-tax basis equal to the conversion amount.
Wrong (B): There is no rule that taxes half of a conversion; the taxable amount depends on the ratio of pre-tax to total IRA balances.
Wrong (C): There are no income limits on Roth conversions; the conversion is permitted regardless of AGI.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4060;

-- Q4083: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["100% of the current year tax liability paid in four equal installments","100% of the prior year tax liability paid in four equal installments","The lesser of 100% of the current year or 100% of the prior year liability","90% of the current year tax liability as the sole safe harbor amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 6655(d)(1), a corporation must make estimated payments equal to the lesser of 100% of the current year''s liability or 100% of the prior year''s liability to avoid the underpayment penalty. For non-large corporations, either safe harbor applies to all four installments.
Wrong (A): 100% of the current year liability is one safe harbor, but the corporation can alternatively use 100% of the prior year — the requirement is the lesser of both.
Wrong (B): 100% of the prior year liability is also a safe harbor, but the rule specifies the lesser of both options, not this one alone.
Wrong (D): The 90% threshold applies to individuals, not corporations; corporations use the 100%/100% lesser-of test under Sec. 6655.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4083;

-- Q4118: International Tax [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As ordinary income taxed entirely in the current distribution year","Allocated ratably over the holding period, taxed at the highest rate plus interest","As long-term capital gain eligible for preferential tax rates","Tax-free under the participation exemption for corporate shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the default PFIC rules of Section 1291, excess distributions are allocated ratably over the shareholder''s holding period. Amounts allocated to prior years are taxed at the highest rate in effect for each year, plus an interest charge for the deferral period. Only the current-year portion is taxed at current ordinary rates.
Wrong (A): The distribution is not taxed entirely in the current year; it is allocated across the entire holding period under the excess distribution rules.
Wrong (C): PFIC excess distributions are not treated as capital gains; they are subject to the punitive highest-rate-plus-interest regime.
Wrong (D): The participation exemption (Section 245A) does not apply to PFIC shareholders; it applies to dividends from specified 10%-owned foreign corporations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4118;

-- Q4119: International Tax [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The mark-to-market election under Section 1296","The check-the-box election under entity classification rules","The Section 962 election for individual CFC shareholders","The qualified electing fund election under Section 1295"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A QEF election under Section 1295 allows the US shareholder to include annually their pro rata share of the PFIC''s ordinary earnings and net capital gain. This current-inclusion regime avoids the interest charge and highest-rate taxation of the default excess distribution method. The PFIC must provide an annual information statement.
Wrong (A): The mark-to-market election under Section 1296 requires recognizing annual gain or loss based on FMV changes, not including the PFIC''s actual earnings.
Wrong (B): The check-the-box election changes entity classification but does not provide for annual income inclusion from a PFIC.
Wrong (C): Section 962 allows individuals to elect corporate tax rates on CFC inclusions, but it does not address PFIC excess distribution rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4119;

-- Q4132: International Tax [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No tax consequence because intercompany loans are excluded from Subpart F","A deemed dividend inclusion up to the CFC''s earnings and profits under Section 956","The CFC recognizes capital gain on the principal amount of the loan","The loan is recharacterized as an equity contribution to the US parent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A CFC''s loan to its US parent is an investment in US property under Section 956. The US parent must include in income the lesser of the CFC''s average investment in US property or its accumulated earnings and profits not previously taxed. This rule prevents tax-free repatriation of CFC earnings through loans.
Wrong (A): Intercompany loans from a CFC to its US shareholder are specifically targeted by Section 956 and are not excluded from Subpart F consequences.
Wrong (C): The CFC does not recognize capital gain on a loan; the consequence falls on the US shareholder as a deemed dividend inclusion.
Wrong (D): The loan is not recharacterized as equity; it is treated as an investment in US property triggering income inclusion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4132;

-- Q4142: International Tax [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Foreign base company sales income under Section 954(d)","Foreign base company services income under Section 954(e)","Foreign personal holding company income under Section 954(c)","Insurance income as defined under Section 953 of the Code"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 954(c), foreign personal holding company income (FPHCI) includes dividends, interest, royalties, rents, annuities, and net gains from property producing such income. FPHCI is a major component of foreign base company income, which is a category of Subpart F income targeting passive investment income.
Wrong (A): Foreign base company sales income under Sec. 954(d) involves sales of personal property bought from or sold to a related person, not passive investment income.
Wrong (B): Foreign base company services income under Sec. 954(e) involves services performed for or on behalf of a related person, not dividends and interest.
Wrong (D): Insurance income under Sec. 953 involves income from insuring risks outside the CFC''s country of organization, not passive investment income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4142;

-- Q4151: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A constructive dividend to the shareholder and deemed interest income to the corporation","A taxable gift from the corporation to the shareholder under gift tax rules","A capital contribution from the shareholder back to the issuing corporation","A nontaxable return of capital reducing the shareholder''s stock basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 7872(a), below-market loans between a corporation and its shareholder are recharacterized. The forgone interest ($150,000 x 4% = $6,000) is treated as a constructive dividend from the corporation to the shareholder, and a deemed interest payment from the shareholder back to the corporation. Both amounts are recognized for tax purposes.
Wrong (B): Corporation-to-shareholder transfers are treated as dividends, not gifts; gift tax rules apply to individual-to-individual transfers.
Wrong (C): A capital contribution runs from shareholder to corporation, which is the opposite direction of the economic benefit flowing here.
Wrong (D): A below-market loan creates taxable income under Sec. 7872; it is not treated as a nontaxable return of capital.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4151;

-- Q4153: Owner-Entity Transactions [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$30,000 recognized as an ordinary loss on the sale of equipment","$30,000 recognized as a capital loss on the sale of equipment","$0 because the loss is disallowed under Section 267 related-party rules","$15,000 as a partially limited loss based on the ownership percentage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(a)(1), losses on sales between a taxpayer and a corporation in which the taxpayer owns more than 50% are disallowed. Since Tom owns 60% of Alpha Corp, the $30,000 loss ($50,000 - $80,000) is completely disallowed. If Alpha later sells the equipment at a gain, the gain may be reduced by the previously disallowed amount.
Wrong (A): No ordinary loss is recognized because Section 267 disallows the entire loss between related parties.
Wrong (B): No capital loss is recognized either; Section 267 disallows the loss regardless of its character.
Wrong (D): Section 267 does not partially limit the loss based on ownership percentage; the loss is fully disallowed when ownership exceeds 50%.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4153;

-- Q4156: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sale of stock by a shareholder to an unrelated corporation for cash","Sale of depreciable equipment by a majority partner to the partnership","Sale of non-depreciable land between related parties at fair market value","Sale of inventory between unrelated parties in the ordinary course"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary income on sales of depreciable property between related parties. A partner owning more than 50% of a partnership is a related party. Since equipment is depreciable by the partnership, the gain is recharacterized as ordinary income rather than capital gain.
Wrong (A): Stock sales to unrelated corporations do not involve related parties and are not sales of depreciable property, so Section 1239 does not apply.
Wrong (C): Land is not depreciable property, so even between related parties, Section 1239 does not apply to land sales.
Wrong (D): Sales between unrelated parties are outside the scope of Section 1239, which requires a related-party relationship.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4156;

-- Q4162: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No, a profits interest for services is generally nontaxable under Rev. Proc. 93-27","Yes, the partner must recognize $200,000 as ordinary compensation income","Yes, but the amount received is characterized and taxed as capital gain","No, because all partnership interest transfers are automatically tax-free events"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Rev. Proc. 93-27, receipt of a profits interest for services is generally not a taxable event, provided the interest is not disposed of within two years, is not related to a substantially certain income stream, and is not a disguised payment under Sec. 707(a)(2)(A). A profits interest entitles the partner only to future appreciation and income.
Wrong (B): A profits interest has no liquidation value at grant, so there is no immediate income recognition under Sec. 83(a), unlike a capital interest.
Wrong (C): Capital gain treatment does not apply; the question is whether receipt itself is taxable, and under Rev. Proc. 93-27 it is not.
Wrong (D): Not all partnership interest receipts are tax-free; a capital interest received for services is taxable at FMV under Sec. 83(a).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4162;

-- Q4163: Owner-Entity Transactions [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disallowed under Section 267 because Lisa controls more than 50% of both corporations","Fully recognized as an ordinary loss deductible against Lisa''s operating income","Deferred until Beta Corp sells the inventory to an unrelated third party","Recognized but limited to the $3,000 annual capital loss deduction ceiling"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 267(a)(1) disallows losses on sales between an individual and a corporation in which the individual owns more than 50%. Since Lisa owns 80% of Beta Corp, the $15,000 loss is disallowed entirely. The constructive ownership rules under Section 267(c) may also apply to attribute additional ownership.
Wrong (B): The loss cannot be recognized as an ordinary loss because Section 267 disallows it entirely between related parties.
Wrong (C): Section 267 disallows the loss outright rather than deferring it; this differs from Section 1091 wash sale rules or Section 267(d) buyer benefit rules.
Wrong (D): The $3,000 capital loss limitation under Sec. 1211(b) is irrelevant because the loss is never recognized in the first place.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4163;

-- Q4171: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["7.65%, representing only the employee share of FICA taxes","12.4% applied to the full $200,000 guaranteed payment amount","21%, matching the current flat corporate income tax rate","15.3% on the first $168,600 and 2.9% on the remaining excess"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1402(a) and Sec. 707(c), guaranteed payments for services are subject to self-employment tax. The combined SE rate under Sec. 1401 is 15.3% — 12.4% Social Security tax on earnings up to the wage base ($168,600) and 2.9% Medicare tax with no cap. Above the wage base, only 2.9% Medicare applies.
Wrong (A): 7.65% is the employee-only share of FICA; self-employed individuals pay both the employer and employee shares, totaling 15.3%.
Wrong (B): The 12.4% Social Security component applies only up to the wage base, not on all amounts; Medicare at 2.9% continues above the cap.
Wrong (C): 21% is the corporate income tax rate under Sec. 11(b), not the self-employment tax rate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4171;

-- Q4175: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No effect because only stock basis is relevant for S corporation loss deductions","Decreases Nancy''s stock basis by the $200,000 loan principal amount","Increases Nancy''s stock basis by the $200,000 loan principal amount","Creates $200,000 of debt basis allowing additional loss deductions beyond stock basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1)(B), direct shareholder loans to an S corporation create debt basis that allows loss deductions beyond stock basis. Nancy''s $200,000 loan creates debt basis. Losses first reduce stock basis under Sec. 1367(a)(2), and once stock basis is zero, additional losses reduce debt basis.
Wrong (A): Both stock basis and debt basis are relevant; Sec. 1366(d)(1) allows losses to reduce debt basis after stock basis is exhausted.
Wrong (B): A shareholder loan does not decrease stock basis; it creates a separate debt basis that is tracked independently.
Wrong (C): Direct loans create debt basis, not stock basis; only capital contributions and income allocations increase stock basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4175;

-- Q4178: Owner-Entity Transactions [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["February 28 of the following year, when the cash-method partner includes it in income","December 31 of the current year, when the partnership accrues the expense","Ratably over the 12-month period to which the rent payment relates","The rent deduction is permanently disallowed under the related-party rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 267(a)(2), when an accrual-method partnership owes amounts to a cash-method related-party partner, the partnership cannot deduct the expense until the partner includes it in income. Since the partner reports the rent when received on February 28, the partnership''s deduction is deferred to that date.
Wrong (B): The partnership cannot deduct on December 31 because the cash-method partner has not yet included the payment in income at that point.
Wrong (C): Section 267(a)(2) does not provide for ratable deduction; the deduction is deferred entirely until the payee includes it in income.
Wrong (D): The deduction is not permanently disallowed — it is merely deferred until the partner reports the income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4178;

-- Q4194: Advanced Basis Calculations [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The total fair market value of all corporate assets at year end","The cumulative undistributed S corporation income previously taxed to shareholders","The corporation''s retained earnings balance calculated under GAAP standards","The aggregate total of all capital contributions made by the shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 1368(e)(1), the AAA tracks cumulative S corporation income that has been taxed to shareholders but not yet distributed. AAA determines whether distributions come from previously taxed S income (tax-free return of basis) or from accumulated E&P (taxable as dividends under Sec. 1368(c)).
Wrong (A): AAA is a tax account tracking taxed but undistributed income, not a measure of the corporation''s total asset value.
Wrong (C): Retained earnings is a GAAP concept for financial reporting; AAA is a tax-only construct with different adjustment rules.
Wrong (D): Capital contributions are not tracked through AAA; they affect stock basis directly and are separate from income-based adjustments.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4194;

-- Q4217: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because Janet transferred qualifying property to the corporation","Yes, because Janet and Kim together own 100% of the outstanding stock","No, because Janet alone owns only 70% and fails the 80% control test","No, because Kim''s service contribution disqualifies the entire transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 351 requires that transferors of property control the corporation (80% or more) immediately after the exchange. Kim contributed only services, not property, so Kim is excluded from the control group. Janet alone owns 70%, which is below the 80% threshold, so she must recognize her $100,000 gain.
Wrong (A): Transferring property is necessary but not sufficient; the transferor must also be part of a control group owning at least 80%.
Wrong (B): Combined ownership is irrelevant because Kim is not counted as a transferor of property — only property transferors count toward the 80% test.
Wrong (D): Kim''s service contribution does not disqualify the transaction entirely; it simply excludes Kim from the control group calculation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4217;

-- Q4231: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because both property transferors together own 95% exceeding the 80% threshold","Yes, because each transferor individually owns more than 50% of the stock","No, because neither transferor individually meets the 80% control requirement","No, because the receipt of boot cash disqualifies the entire exchange"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 351 looks at aggregate control by all transferors of property. Carol and Dave together own 95% (50% + 45%), exceeding the 80% threshold. The receipt of boot ($5,000 cash to Dave) does not disqualify the transaction; Dave simply recognizes gain to the extent of boot received.
Wrong (B): Section 351 uses aggregate ownership of all property transferors, not individual ownership; neither needs to own over 50%.
Wrong (C): The 80% test is applied to the group of property transferors collectively, not to each individual transferor.
Wrong (D): Receipt of boot does not disqualify a Section 351 exchange; the transferor receiving boot recognizes gain only to the extent of the boot.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4231;

-- Q4238: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$10,000 loss because the total distribution was less than outside basis","$0 gain or loss because cash received did not exceed the partner''s outside basis","$10,000 gain because the distribution exceeded the property''s inside basis","$30,000 gain equal to the total cash amount received by the partner"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 731, gain is recognized only if cash exceeds the partner''s outside basis. The $30,000 cash does not exceed the $60,000 outside basis, so no gain is recognized. Loss is recognized in a liquidating distribution only if the partner receives solely cash, unrealized receivables, or inventory — since property was also distributed, no loss is recognized.
Wrong (A): Loss cannot be recognized because the partner received property in addition to cash; loss requires receipt of only cash, receivables, or inventory.
Wrong (C): Gain requires cash exceeding outside basis under Section 731; the property''s inside basis is not the relevant comparison.
Wrong (D): The $30,000 cash does not trigger $30,000 of gain because it is less than the $60,000 outside basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4238;

-- Q4243: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ordinary income reported on the retiring partner''s individual tax return","Guaranteed payments subject to self-employment tax obligations","Section 731 distributions resulting in capital gain or loss treatment","Self-employment income subject to FICA and Medicare tax withholding"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 736(b), payments for a retiring partner''s interest in partnership property are treated as distributions governed by Section 731. This means they result in capital gain to the extent cash exceeds basis, or capital loss in a complete liquidation when only cash, receivables, or inventory are received.
Wrong (A): Section 736(b) payments are not ordinary income; they receive distribution treatment with capital gain or loss character under Section 731.
Wrong (B): Guaranteed payments apply to Section 736(a) payments for services or unrealized receivables, not Section 736(b) property payments.
Wrong (D): Self-employment income characterization applies to Section 736(a) payments, not to Section 736(b) distributions for partnership property.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4243;

-- Q4284: Entity Choice and Planning [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Automatic limited liability protection for all partners in the entity","Unlimited classes of ownership interests with varying economic rights","Lower overall self-employment tax burden on partnership distributions","Ability to make special allocations of income and loss among partners"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 704(b), partnerships can make special allocations of income, gain, loss, deduction, and credit among partners, provided they have substantial economic effect. S corporations cannot make special allocations — income must be allocated pro rata based on stock ownership under Section 1366(a).
Wrong (A): General partnerships do not provide limited liability for all partners; only limited partnerships and LLCs offer that, and S corporations also provide limited liability.
Wrong (B): While partnerships do allow flexible ownership classes, S corporations are limited to one class of stock — but this answer overstates the advantage since both can have varying economic rights through different mechanisms.
Wrong (C): Partnerships often result in higher self-employment taxes for general partners compared to S corporation shareholder-employees who can limit SE tax through reasonable salary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4284;
